<GuideLayout
  title="Planning an overnight hike in the Blue Mountains"
  seoTitle="Overnight Hikes in the Blue Mountains: Routes and Fire Rules"
  pageImage={guideImage}
  pageImageAlt="Grose River near Blue Gum Forest in the Grose Valley, Blue Mountains"
  description="Overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains: verified NPWS routes, walk-in campsites, booking rules and fire season limits for planning your first multi-day trip."
  eyebrow="Where to go"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>
    The Blue Mountains is the obvious first overnight hike for most Sydney
    beginners: an hour or two by car or train, sandstone cliffs and eucalypt
    valleys instead of suburbs, and a handful of walk-in campsites that don't
    demand alpine gear or a week off work.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is also easy to get wrong. Some of these tracks descend hundreds of
    metres of steep, exposed steps that feel very different with a full pack on
    your back, and NSW's fire season closes tracks and restricts stoves for
    months at a time. This guide sticks to routes and rules we can verify
    against NSW National Parks and the NSW Rural Fire Service, and stays out of
    the cooking and gear detail that <a href="/guides/hiking-stove-guide/"
      >our stove guide</a
    > and <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">packing list</a> already
    cover.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Acacia Flat and Ingar campgrounds need a free online booking (a small
        booking fee applies); Kedumba River Crossing needs the same. Perrys
        Lookdown campground cannot be reserved.
      </li>
      <li>
        Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest is a good first overnight hike: 4km
        return, but a steep, "hard" grade descent and climb.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Six Foot Track (Katoomba to Jenolan) is a 42km, three-day step up
        for once you have an overnight hike under your belt, and currently ends
        at Binda Bush cabins while Jenolan Caves is closed.
      </li>
      <li>
        NSW's Bush Fire Danger Period runs statewide from 1 October, and on a
        declared Total Fire Ban day, open fires and solid-fuel cooking are
        banned outright.
      </li>
      <li>
        Always check the specific track's NPWS alerts page and the RFS Total
        Fire Ban status the afternoon before you leave.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figure class="guide-photo">
    <Image
      src={guideImage}
      alt="Grose River near Blue Gum Forest in the Grose Valley, Blue Mountains"
      widths={[360, 720, 1024, 1400]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 680px"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Photo: <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grose_River,_near_Blue_Gum_Forest,_Grose_Valley,_Blue_Mountains_1.JPG"
        rel="noopener"
        >Grose River, near Blue Gum Forest, Grose Valley, Blue Mountains</a
      > by Rangasyd, <a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/"
        rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a
      >, via Wikimedia Commons (resized).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    "Best" depends on your fitness and how comfortable you are on steep, uneven
    track. The four routes below are all inside Blue Mountains National Park,
    all have a verifiable NPWS page, and cover a spread from a manageable first
    overnight hike to a serious three-day walk.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
